openairplay2-receiver (0.5.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  The now-playing display works on-device with no configuration: the
  daemon now serves its WebSocket on a local Unix socket,
  /run/openairplay2/tui.sock, by default. Any local user can run
  openairplay2-tui with no arguments on the same machine and see what is
  playing — the socket file's permissions are the access control, so
  there is no port and no password. OPENAIRPLAY2_TUI_SOCKET names
  another path, or "off" to not serve it. The TCP endpoint
  (OPENAIRPLAY2_TUI_LISTEN, for a display on another machine) is
  unchanged.

  The pairing pincode is now called the password — Apple's own word: iOS
  and macOS show a password dialog for it, and it may be alphanumeric,
  not just digits. Set OPENAIRPLAY2_PASSWORD in the options file;
  OPENAIRPLAY2_PINCODE (and --pincode) from 0.4 are still honored, with
  a reminder in the log to rename.

 -- Stefan Arentz <stefan.arentz@gmail.com>  Sat, 08 Aug 2026 22:00:00 -0400

openairplay2-receiver (0.4.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  The options file /etc/default/openairplay2-receiver no longer uses
  OPENAIRPLAY2_ARGS. Every option is now its own variable, read by the
  daemon itself: OPENAIRPLAY2_NAME, OPENAIRPLAY2_ALSA_DEVICE,
  OPENAIRPLAY2_PINCODE, OPENAIRPLAY2_PORT, OPENAIRPLAY2_AVAHI,
  OPENAIRPLAY2_AUDIO, OPENAIRPLAY2_MAC and OPENAIRPLAY2_TUI_LISTEN — see
  the comments in the shipped options file for each one's meaning and
  default.

  dpkg keeps your locally edited options file on upgrade, so if it still
  sets OPENAIRPLAY2_ARGS, the daemon ignores it, logs an error saying so,
  and runs with its defaults until you migrate the settings to the named
  variables. Moving options into variables is also what keeps a pincode
  out of the world-readable command line (ps).

  A name may now contain %h, which becomes the machine's hostname.

  The now-playing WebSocket (OPENAIRPLAY2_TUI_LISTEN) can now require a
  password: set OPENAIRPLAY2_TUI_PASSWORD, and start openairplay2-tui with
  --password. Without one it remains open to anyone who can reach it, as
  before.

 -- Stefan Arentz <stefan.arentz@gmail.com>  Fri, 07 Aug 2026 22:00:00 -0400
